WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.620
Add interferon to human cells, and genes switch on within minutes without new

2
00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:08.620
protein synthesis.

3
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:13.180
How can a receptor with no enzyme reach DNA that fast?

4
00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:18.120
Every part of the relay must already sit inside the cell, waiting.

5
00:00:19.283 --> 00:00:25.363
Interferon is a cytokine, a small secreted protein carrying messages between cells.

6
00:00:26.683 --> 00:00:31.443
Each receptor chain crosses the membrane once and has no enzyme of its own.

7
00:00:32.743 --> 00:00:37.003
Instead, its inner tail holds a separate tyrosine kinase, called a JAK.

8
00:00:38.303 --> 00:00:41.143
That kinase waits there before any cytokine arrives.

9
00:00:42.233 --> 00:00:47.653
Each JAK has two lobes: a working kinase and a pseudokinase, and an inactive

10
00:00:47.653 --> 00:00:49.753
look-alike that holds the real kinase back.

11
00:00:51.053 --> 00:00:55.633
When a cytokine binds outside, it brings two receptor chains side by side.

12
00:00:56.873 --> 00:01:01.693
Their JAKs now reach across, phosphorylate each other, and switch both kinases fully

13
00:01:01.693 --> 00:01:05.033
on. The receptor has borrowed an enzyme.

14
00:01:06.117 --> 00:01:11.717
Next, the active JAKs add phosphate tags to tyrosines along the receptor tails.

15
00:01:13.117 --> 00:01:15.717
Those phosphotyrosines act as landing pads.

16
00:01:17.057 --> 00:01:20.497
A waiting protein called a STAT drifts up from the cytoplasm.

17
00:01:21.897 --> 00:01:28.037
Using its SH2 domain, a pocket that grips phosphotyrosine, the STAT docks beside the

18
00:01:28.037 --> 00:01:29.037
active JAK.

19
00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:34.127
The JAK adds one phosphate to a single tyrosine near each STAT's tail.

20
00:01:35.607 --> 00:01:36.607
Notice what has changed.

21
00:01:37.907 --> 00:01:41.947
Each STAT already used its SH2 domain to dock on the receptor.

22
00:01:43.327 --> 00:01:47.987
Now each carries its own phosphotyrosine, so what can two tagged STATs do next?

23
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.720
They trade one grip for two.

24
00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:01.360
Each STAT's SH2 domain locks onto its partner's phosphotyrosine.

25
00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:04.440
Receptor docking is reversible.

26
00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:11.000
Once tagged, two reciprocal grips hold the active pair together, replacing one grip

27
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.940
on the tail. The same SH2 domain works twice.

28
00:02:16.033 --> 00:02:19.353
Many STATs already rest in loose, inactive pairs.

29
00:02:20.773 --> 00:02:23.793
Phosphorylation flips them into this tight, active clamp.

30
00:02:24.973 --> 00:02:30.513
The active pair moves into the nucleus and clamps onto a specific DNA sequence to

31
00:02:30.513 --> 00:02:37.113
switch on genes. For some interferons, a third protein, IRF9, joins the pair.

32
00:02:38.167 --> 00:02:42.067
Step back and count the relay stages between receptor and gene.

33
00:02:43.327 --> 00:02:46.407
There is no second messenger and no chain of kinases.

34
00:02:47.807 --> 00:02:52.707
One borrowed kinase marks a waiting transcription factor that travels straight to

35
00:02:52.707 --> 00:02:58.467
DNA. Cytokine receptors borrow JAKs to send paired STATs straight to DNA.

36
00:02:59.667 --> 00:03:03.047
This direct design serves most cytokine receptors.

37
00:03:04.407 --> 00:03:08.467
Both the borrowed kinase and the waiting STAT sit ready inside the cell.

38
00:03:09.747 --> 00:03:14.967
No new protein needs to be built first, so target genes can answer a surface signal

39
00:03:14.967 --> 00:03:15.627
within minutes.
